# Defanged reconstruction of a real malicious package, for scanner tests.
# Never extract and run. See internal/e2e/livescan/corpus.go.
id: ctx-0.2.6
title: "ctx 0.2.6 (PyPI): hijacked package exfiltrating environment variables"
campaign: ctx-account-takeover-2022
date: "2022-05-21"
kind: sdist
ecosystem: pip
name: ctx
version: "0.2.6"
benignVersion: "0.1.2"
summary: >-
  ctx, a small dictionary-with-attribute-access helper last released in 2014,
  was taken over on 2022-05-14 after its maintainer's e-mail domain expired:
  the attacker registered the domain, reset the PyPI password (the account had
  no MFA) and over a week published 18 malicious files (0.1.2-1 … 0.2.6) before
  PyPI removed them on 2022-05-24, about 27,000 downloads later. Their Ctx
  constructor called sendRequest(), which joined every environment variable's
  value, base64-encoded the result and sent it in the path of a GET request to
  a Heroku app (an earlier variant collected the AWS key pair and the host
  name). The same actor hijacked the PHP package phpass the same way.
techniques: [account-takeover, runtime-import, credential-theft, env-exfiltration, base64-encoding]
reconstruction: >-
  ctx.py follows the PyPI incident report's verbatim excerpt: a Ctx dict
  subclass whose __init__ calls sendRequest(), which concatenates every value
  of os.environ, base64-encodes it and appends it to the GET URL; the AWS key
  pair lookup of the earlier variant is folded in. The exfiltration host is
  defanged (".invalid" appended). The rest of the module is a small stand-in
  for the original helper.
references:
  - https://python-security.readthedocs.io/pypi-vuln/index-2022-05-24-ctx-domain-takeover.html
  - https://isc.sans.edu/diary/28678
  - https://www.sonatype.com/blog/pypi-package-ctx-compromised-are-you-at-risk
  - https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/popular-python-and-php-libraries-hijacked-to-steal-aws-keys/
indicators:
  - anti-theft-web
  - sendRequest
-- shared/setup.py --
from setuptools import setup

setup(
    name="ctx",
    version=__import__("ctx").__version__,
    description="A minimal Python dictionary with dot-notation access",
    long_description=open("README.rst").read(),
    py_modules=["ctx"],
    license="MIT",
    classifiers=[
        "License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License",
        "Programming Language :: Python :: 3",
    ],
)
-- shared/README.rst --
ctx
===

``Ctx`` is a ``dict`` whose keys can also be read and written as attributes::

    >>> from ctx import Ctx
    >>> c = Ctx(name="demo")
    >>> c.name
    'demo'
